---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { emph } from "../lib/text.js";
import tna from "../data/tna-discovery.json";
import naval from "../data/naval-devon-taylor-brayley.json";

/* Both files are rendered here rather than summarised, because a finding that lives only in
   data/ is a finding the reader never sees - which is the whole fault this archive spent
   17 September 2026 fixing. If either TSV gains a row, this page gains it too. */
const cols = (r) => Object.values(r);
---
<Base title="The British archives answer"
      eyebrow="The UK National Archives"
      dek="On 17 September 2026 the UK National Archives was searched for the first time in this project. The Lerena surname is absent from Britain. The wife's line is not: the Brayleys fill 639 records, and five men of Northam and Bideford came back with their exact dates of birth.">

<section class="blk">
  <div class="panel" style="margin:1.4rem 0">
    <p style="margin:0">
      <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>Discovery</strong>, the catalogue of
      <a href="https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/" rel="noopener">The National Archives</a>,
      searched 17 September 2026. It is free and needs no account, and it aggregates roughly
      <strong>2,500 UK repositories</strong> rather than Kew alone &mdash; on the control query,
      Kew held 4,791 records and other archives 11,719, most of them the
      <strong>North Devon Record Office</strong>.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:.7rem 0 0">
      <strong>The control was run first.</strong> <span class="mono">Bideford</span> returns
      <strong>16,510</strong> records, so every zero on this page is a tested zero rather than a
      search that failed.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-surname">The surname is absent from Britain</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>Lerena returns two records, and neither is a person of this family</strong> &mdash; an
    18th-century street plan of Seville dedicated to a Spanish finance minister, and a 1915 crew list
    that matches incidentally. <em>Llerena</em> returns one Peninsular War letter about the Spanish
    town of that name. <em>Lerina</em> returns two, both incidental.</p>
    <p>This is what it should look like. A family that went from Rosario to the Cape without ever
    touching Britain leaves nothing in British archives, and now that has been tested rather than
    assumed.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-what">What was searched, and what came back</h2>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr>
        <th style="width:12rem">Query</th><th style="width:4rem">Count</th>
        <th style="width:20rem">What it holds</th><th>Verdict</th>
      </tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {tna.map((r) => {
          const c = cols(r);
          return (
            <tr>
              <th set:html={emph(c[0])} />
              <td><strong>{c[1]}</strong></td>
              <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[2])} />
              <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[3])} />
            </tr>
          );
        })}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-naval">The naval sweep, and a near miss that is now closed</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Northam parish contains <strong>Appledore</strong>, so if the Taylor men went to sea the Royal
    Navy registers should hold them. <span class="mono">ADM 188</span> is indexed man by man and gives
    an <strong>exact date of birth</strong>, which makes it testable against a tree instead of merely
    suggestive.</p>
    <p>It returned five men of these two parishes. <strong>Not one of them is a man of this
    family</strong> &mdash; and that the method found five is precisely what makes the absence of
    William John and Horace Archibald Taylor a tested null.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr>
        <th style="width:12rem">Name</th><th style="width:10rem">Born</th>
        <th style="width:20rem">Record</th><th>Verdict</th>
      </tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {naval.map((r) => {
          const c = cols(r);
          return (
            <tr>
              <th set:html={emph(c[0])} />
              <td set:html={emph(c[1])} />
              <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[2])} />
              <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[3])} />
            </tr>
          );
        })}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-cost">What is free, and what is not</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The part that does the genealogical work &mdash; <strong>name, exact date of birth, birthplace,
    year of entry</strong> &mdash; is free to anyone, with no account. The scanned record behind it is
    &pound;3.50, or <strong>free to a signed-in National Archives account</strong>, and shows ships,
    conduct, physical description and pension.</p>
    <p><strong>Downloads are licensed for private study or non-commercial research.</strong> This site
    is public, so the line taken here is to <strong>cite the reference and transcribe the facts, and
    never republish the scan</strong>. Facts carry no copyright; the image is licensed. Download links
    also expire thirty days from the order, so anything wanted for good must be read and written down
    inside that window.</p>
    <p class="dim">Related: <a href={u("/taylor/")}>The Taylors</a> &middot;
      <a href={u("/sources/")}>Sources</a> &middot;
      <a href={u("/worklist/")}>The work list</a> &middot;
      <a href={u("/searched/")}>What has been read</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

</Base>
